  "p1_b4": {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "bbox": [
    252.5,
    726.4,
    726.4,
    888.1
   ],
   "ocr": "72, 24, 24 and 8 h of contact time were required to remove all initial Cr(VI) at 15, 28, 35 and 45°C, respectively. Fig 4b shows that temperature also affected total Cr removal by CLB, but to a lesser extent than for Cr(VI). CLB removed all initial Cr(VI) at temperatures from 15 to 45°C, but was not able to remove the entire total Cr. Residual total Cr concentration was considerably higher at all temperatures and contact times than residual Cr(VI) concentration. As mentioned before, this was due to Cr(VI) which was reduced to Cr(III) by CLB and released into the aque- ous solution (Fig 4c). The highest Cr(III) levels were produced at 45°C, and decreased as temperature fell (Fig 4c). Thus, reduction of Cr(VI) to Cr(III) by CLB was favored at high temperatures."
  },
